@@ -14,3 +14,15 @@ pointer returns the whole document. Throws `JsonPointerError` if the pointer is
 canonical form, the `-` token (section 4: nonexistent element), or traversal into a primitive. Only
 own members are looked up, so `__proto__`, `constructor` etc. never resolve to inherited values.
 The document is never modified.
+
+### `applyPatch(document, patch)`
+
+Implements JSON Patch (RFC 6902) with its six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`
+and `test`. Paths are JSON Pointers (RFC 6901). Returns the patched document as a new value; neither
+`document` nor `patch` is modified, and a patch that fails has no effect (section 5). Throws
+`JsonPatchError` if the patch is not an array of valid operations (unknown `op`, missing or malformed
+`path`/`from`/`value`), or if an operation fails: missing target, bad array index, `move` from a proper
+prefix of its path, or a `test` that does not match (JSON equality, section 4.6). Unrecognized members
+of an operation are ignored. Removing the whole document (`remove` with the empty path) is rejected,
+as JSON has no value for "nothing". Keys such as `__proto__` are handled as ordinary own members and
+never pollute prototypes.
